Subject: [PATCH 2/2] UI streamlining pass: eleven screens
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"Due to adding too many features and not knowing where they're supposed to
go the ui is looking more and more confusing." One screen at a time,
Dashboard first and then down the sidebar. Each screen was offered as three
designs and built once picked: Dashboard A, Inbox B, New Task B, All Tasks C,
Contracts C, Data B, Marketing Inbox B, Clients+Vendors A, Tracking A then B.
Team and Settings were one cleanup each. CRM keeps its layout; only its bugs
are fixed.

Every screen's logic moved into a pure lib/ module — no React, no Supabase,
no argless new Date() — so the rules are testable and the same date renders
on the server and in the browser. 662 unit tests and 300-odd browser tests
drive the real components.

WHAT WAS ACTUALLY BROKEN

  An unguarded delete on Marketing Inbox: a ✕ ten pixels from the thing you
  click to open, calling deleteTask with no question. A miss by a centimetre
  destroyed the campaign, its subtasks and their notifications.

  The Zoho "Import" button deleted every client in the workspace on
  Ctrl/Cmd+click, documented only in a title tooltip.

  Six unpaged reads of tables that grow past PostgREST's 1000-row cap, which
  truncates silently: workspace stats, the campaign rollup, contract
  requests, tracking rollups, member task counts, lookup usage.

  Cancelled contract requests appeared on no screen at all — five statuses,
  four rendered.

  Settings' delete confirmation said the opposite of what the database does.
  It claimed rows keep their value; every FK is `on delete set null`, so
  deleting a category silently blanked it on every campaign and client.

  Nobody below admin could edit their own name: MyProfileCard lived on
  Settings, which is visibleTo ['owner','admin'].

  CRM's "Dormant contacts" was upside down. Last-contact came from the 500
  most recent activities, so a contact whose newest note was the 501st read
  as never contacted — and never sorts first. The accounts we speak to most
  were listed as the ones nobody had called. Its stat card also couldn't
  count past six, reading the length of a list already sliced for display.

  Open work was double-counted (assignee AND task_members on one task) and
  counted cancelled campaigns, on both Team and the Dashboard's Workload.

  A finished campaign was counted as late. Unpriced work showed 0.00, which
  says free. Tracking's row numbers renumbered under a filter — the number
  people read out on a call. Export Excel silently exported only the filtered
  rows. A role change was a select's onChange, no confirmation, on a
  permissions change.

TRACKING SHEETS, AND WHY 063

  tracking_rows and vendor_ad_lines hold the same ad in two tables, and
  ensureTrackingRowForVendor was idempotent by vendor NAME — so a vendor
  booked for six home ads and six store visits got twelve ad lines and
  exactly one sheet row, carrying one posting date and one status for all
  twelve. Migration 063 keys rows to (ad_line_id, ad_line_seq): one row per
  ad. Nothing is backfilled; the sheet says how many booked ads are missing
  and adds them only when asked.

  063 also changes tracking_rows_published. publish_tracking_sheet() does
  `insert ... select r.*`, which is positional — adding a column to one
  table and not the other breaks publishing for every campaign. The
  migration ends with a check that raises if the column counts diverge.

  MIGRATION 063 MUST RUN BEFORE THIS DEPLOYS.

RULES THIS PASS APPLIES

  One question per screen. Rank, don't tile. Size means importance.
  Never colour alone; colour means state, not quantity. Nothing is a dead
  end. A control is named for where it goes. One button per row, and it is
  the next move. An absent control is indistinguishable from a missing
  feature — draw it disabled with the reason on it. Never two quantities in
  one column. Nothing destructive on a row you skim. A destructive question
  says what the database will actually do. No pre-picked opinions. Zero is
  not a fact about money. A number people say out loud must not move. A
  control that sends something must say what it is sending. The screen named
  for a thing owns that thing.

